## Context Window Guide

The AI/DLC pipeline runs in a single conversation by default.
Context management is governed by SKILL.md Rule 2 and the
Handoff Protocol section in SKILL.md.

**Pipeline snapshot is the primary state vehicle.** The lead
maintains `_bmad-output/pipeline-snapshot.md` throughout the
sprint — created at pipeline start, refreshed at every gate
passage (gate-validation.md Check 14) and at sub-step boundaries
(sub-step snapshot update helper). The snapshot records pipeline
variant, current step, last gate, sprint context, recent
activity, open items, locked decisions, and context-reminder fire
state. When the lead is uncertain about state, it reads the
snapshot, not the conversation scrollback.

**Disk artifacts are secondary state.** Planning outputs live in
`_bmad-output/planning-artifacts/` and are loaded by each step
file as needed. Agent Teams teammates each have their own context
window and load artifacts independently — they do not inherit the
lead's conversation history.

**Context thresholds.** The lead emits two non-blocking reminders
at model-aware absolute token thresholds. Defaults (SKILL.md
Handoff Protocol "Threshold defaults"): 200K model → yellow 80K,
red 120K; 1M model → yellow 120K, red 200K. Projects override by
editing the threshold table in SKILL.md directly. Reminders are
one-line outputs, not pauses — the pipeline continues after the
lead emits each line.

**Reminders recur.** After the first crossing, each reminder
re-fires every 50K tokens OR every 20 turns past the last fire,
whichever comes first. A single early alert is not sufficient for
long sessions.

**Context usage is measured, not estimated.** The
`ai-dlc-context-sensor.sh` Stop hook reads the session transcript
every turn, sums the last main-thread assistant's
`input_tokens + cache_creation_input_tokens + cache_read_input_tokens`
— Claude Code's own context figure — and fires the reminder itself.
The lead does not self-measure and does not guess. You can still
share `/context` output as confirmation, but nothing depends on it.

If your model has a 1M context window, set `AI_DLC_MODEL_ROW` to
`1M` in the `env` block of `.claude/settings.json`. The transcript
does not record the window size, so the sensor assumes the 200K
thresholds until it observes a reading no 200K model could reach.
Pinning it avoids one session of early reminders.

**Hitting limits — formal handoff.** When context is degrading,
use the Rule 2(a) handoff procedure: the lead commits in-flight
work, finalizes the snapshot, outputs a pasteable resume prompt,
and ends the session. Resume in a new conversation by pasting
that resume prompt (beginning with *"Resuming an ai-dlc sprint
from handoff checkpoint. Pipeline snapshot:
`_bmad-output/pipeline-snapshot.md`."*). See SKILL.md Handoff
Protocol section for the full procedure and resume prompt template.

**Auto-handoff (configurable).** The `auto_handoff_mode` setting in
SKILL.md Handoff Protocol "Auto-handoff" section controls whether
the lead automatically executes the Rule 2(a) procedure at defined
safe seams when the sensor has measured the red threshold crossed.
Three modes; edit SKILL.md to change:

- `off` — Never fires. Reminders remain non-blocking; you decide
  when to handoff. Default for new installs.
- `deploy-only` — Fires only at `deploy-validate.md` Step 0
  pre-flight when the existing hard gate cannot be satisfied.
- `safe-seam` — Fires at every safe seam (deploy pre-flight,
  before each gate, after each story transition, between
  adversarial review passes) when all preconditions hold.

Every seam is an EVALUATION point. The shared helper in
`gate-validation.md` checks seven preconditions (mode permits,
red measured, snapshot current, not mid-gate, not mid-deploy, no
teammate waiting, not at a pause point) and fires only when all
hold. Red is set only from a real measurement of resident context;
there is no estimate path, and the sensor stays silent when it
cannot measure.

Auto-handoff is NOT a fourth pause point (the pipeline has only the
three Rule 3 pause points) — it is a session-terminating action. Resume itself remains manual: the
user opens a new conversation and pastes the resume prompt. Auto-
handoff output is distinguishable from a human-requested handoff
via a preceding line naming the mode, seam, and confirmed token
count.

**Recovery from `/compact`.** After a `/compact` or auto-compact
event, the Post-Compact Recovery Protocol (SKILL.md) fires: the
lead re-reads the snapshot, acknowledges recovery, outputs the
verification turn (current step, last gate, in-flight sub-step,
git branch + last commit), and proceeds immediately to the next
pipeline action in the same response. The user may interrupt on
the next turn to correct mismatched state or request handoff.

