## Key Gotchas

**Adversarial review false positives.** The AI will manufacture some
findings. The autonomous loop applies real fixes and discards noise. The
cycle terminates when a pass reports zero CRITICAL and no more than three
blocking MAJOR -- however many passes that takes, including one.

**Token usage.** This workflow prioritizes thoroughness. Expect 5-10x token
usage vs. a standard approach. Break sprints to 3-5 stories.

**File conflicts.** Ownership boundaries in `team-roles/*.md` prevent two
agents editing the same file. The lead enforces this.

**Context loss between teammates.** Teammates do NOT inherit the lead's
history. Everything they need is in files on disk.

**Model verification.** The lead verifies each teammate's model after spawn.

**`--dangerously-skip-permissions` is required for autonomy.** Without
this flag, every file edit and bash command triggers a confirmation prompt.
This breaks the autonomous validation loops (code review --> fix -->
re-review cycles would stall at every write). Always work on a git branch
so you can revert if an agent makes an unexpected change.

**Pipeline interruption.** If the pipeline is interrupted, resume
using the SKILL.md Handoff Protocol resume prompt. In a new
conversation paste: "Resuming an ai-dlc sprint from handoff
checkpoint. Pipeline snapshot: `_bmad-output/pipeline-snapshot.md`."
Do NOT re-invoke `/ai-dlc` with a plain description. The router
treats any input that does not begin with the resume signal as a
fresh pipeline run and archives the existing snapshot. See SKILL.md
Handoff Protocol section for the full resume prompt template.

**BMAD skips to implementation.** If the agent starts coding during a
planning step, the step file instructions should prevent this. If it
happens, interrupt: "This is a planning exercise. Write the output to
planning artifacts. Do not implement."

